WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.874 --> 00:00:13.522
It was risky to attempt to reach Shanghai in that season of the year.

2
00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:19.206
The Chinese seas were dangerous and terrible storms threatened during November.

3
00:00:20.828 --> 00:00:25.832
But John Bunsby believed in the Tankadere, which, in his words, "rode the waves like a seagull."

4
00:00:27.976 --> 00:00:31.818
Late in the day, Fogg and the others sailed out through the channels of Hong Kong.

5
00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:37.140
The Tankadere, encouraged by favorable winds, conducted itself admirably.

6
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.141
John Bunsby had high hopes for the journey and assured Mr.

7
00:00:41.201 --> 00:00:43.042
Fogg that they would reach Shanghai in time.

8
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:50.705
When they got into open sea, Phileas Fogg said, "Captain, I'm sure that I do not need to

9
00:00:50.905 --> 00:00:52.906
ask you to use all possible speed."

10
00:00:53.846 --> 00:00:54.667
"Trust me, Your Honor.

11
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:58.500
We have raised all the sails the wind will allow," said the captain.

12
00:00:59.861 --> 00:01:04.302
Phileas Fogg, with body straight and legs apart, stood like a sailor at the bow of the boat.

13
00:01:05.382 --> 00:01:07.643
He gazed at the swelling waters without staggering.

14
00:01:07.663 --> 00:01:11.784
Aouda, who sat further back, looked out upon the ocean.

15
00:01:13.384 --> 00:01:17.025
The boat, carried forward by the wind, seemed to be flying over the waves.

16
00:01:18.526 --> 00:01:21.467
Fix, seated below, kept apart from his fellow travelers.

17
00:01:22.607 --> 00:01:26.048
He did not like to talk to the man whose favors he had been forced to accept.

18
00:01:27.168 --> 00:01:29.789
"Once in the United States, what should I do?"

19
00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:31.169
he asked himself.

20
00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:33.630
"Should I abandon my man?

21
00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.451
No! A hundred times, no!

22
00:01:37.111 --> 00:01:39.351
I will not lose sight of him for an hour!"

23
00:01:40.252 --> 00:01:43.413
Mr. Fogg and Aouda ate with good appetites that evening.

24
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:47.854
Fix was invited to share their meal, which he accepted reluctantly.

25
00:01:48.954 --> 00:01:53.035
To travel at this man's expense and live upon his provisions was distasteful to him.

26
00:01:53.595 --> 00:01:55.736
Still, he was obliged to eat so he ate.

27
00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.701
When the meal was over, he took Mr.

28
00:01:58.721 --> 00:02:03.625
Fogg aside and said, "Sir, you have been very kind to pay my way on this boat.

29
00:02:04.506 --> 00:02:06.707
But I must ask to pay my share."

30
00:02:06.727 --> 00:02:09.689
"Let us not speak of that, sir," replied Mr.

31
00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:11.811
Fogg. Fix was determined.

32
00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:15.894
"But, I insist—" "No, sir," repeated Mr.

33
00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:19.276
Fogg. "I consider it part of my general expenses."

34
00:02:20.377 --> 00:02:24.040
Fix bowed, returned to his cot, and did not speak again that evening.

35
00:02:25.788 --> 00:02:28.610
At daybreak the next morning, the wind began to blow harder.

36
00:02:30.032 --> 00:02:32.434
The sun had set in a red mist the night before.

37
00:02:33.875 --> 00:02:37.077
John Bunsby had studied ocean weather long enough to know what it meant.

38
00:02:37.097 --> 00:02:41.241
"We are going to have a rough wind," the captain said to Mr.

39
00:02:41.281 --> 00:02:44.103
Fogg. "Is the wind north or south?"

40
00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:45.204
asked Mr.

41
00:02:45.244 --> 00:02:46.625
Fogg. "South.

42
00:02:47.025 --> 00:02:48.567
Look! A typhoon is coming up!"

43
00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:49.708
said the captain.

44
00:02:49.728 --> 00:02:54.992
"I am glad it's a typhoon from the south, as it will carry us forward!"

45
00:02:55.356 --> 00:02:55.817
said Mr.

46
00:02:55.837 --> 00:02:59.779
Fogg. The captain took precautions and tied up all the sails.

47
00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.862
The top masts were put away, and all hands went forward.

48
00:03:05.383 --> 00:03:10.527
A single triangular sail of strong canvas was hoisted at the bow to keep the boat from turning

49
00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:11.148
into the wind.

50
00:03:12.669 --> 00:03:13.689
Then they waited.

51
00:03:15.891 --> 00:03:19.594
John Bunsby had requested that all passengers go below, but Mr.

52
00:03:19.634 --> 00:03:22.356
Fogg, Aouda, and Fix chose to remain on deck.

53
00:03:23.452 --> 00:03:27.654
None of them wanted to feel imprisoned in a narrow space with very little air while the boat

54
00:03:27.694 --> 00:03:28.315
bounced about.

55
00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:32.197
The storm and rain started around eight o'clock.

56
00:03:34.118 --> 00:03:36.939
The Tankadere was lifted like a feather by a violent wind.

57
00:03:41.442 --> 00:03:44.223
The boat flew northward, carried by monstrous waves.

58
00:03:45.104 --> 00:03:50.787
The Tankadere was almost submerged numerous times by mountains of water that rose behind it, but the skill

59
00:03:50.807 --> 00:03:52.088
of the captain saved the boat.

60
00:03:53.676 --> 00:03:57.359
The passengers were often bathed in a cold spray that was whipped by the wind.

61
00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.441
Fix cursed it.

62
00:04:00.161 --> 00:04:03.864
Aouda kept her eyes fastened on her protector, whose coolness amazed her.

63
00:04:04.945 --> 00:04:05.506
As for Mr.

64
00:04:05.546 --> 00:04:08.568
Fogg, he acted as if the typhoon was just part of his plan.

65
00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.491
The Tankadere kept steady on its northward track.

66
00:04:14.633 --> 00:04:17.716
The night was a terror, and it was a miracle that the boat did not sink.

67
00:04:20.664 --> 00:04:24.147
Twice the boat would have gone down if the crew had not been constantly on the watch.

68
00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:28.311
Aouda was exhausted, and more than once, Mr.

69
00:04:28.351 --> 00:04:30.894
Fogg rushed to protect her from the violence of the waves.

70
00:04:32.775 --> 00:04:35.918
Day reappeared and the tempest raged just as fiercely.

71
00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.423
By noon there were signs that the wind was calming, and the night was comparatively quiet.

72
00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:46.268
Some of the sails were raised, and the Tankadere moved forward with speed again.

73
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.622
The next morning at dawn, the passengers could see the coast of Shanghai.

74
00:04:53.604 --> 00:04:55.566
The wind and waves had grown much calmer.

75
00:04:56.606 --> 00:05:02.090
At seven, they were still three miles from Shanghai when a long black smokestack, crowned with a plume

76
00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.672
of white smoke, appeared on the waters near the coast.

77
00:05:04.692 --> 00:05:07.373
"Blast our luck!"

78
00:05:07.734 --> 00:05:08.654
cried John Bunsby.

79
00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:11.156
"The Yokohama steamer has left the port!"

80
00:05:16.212 --> 00:05:17.753
I heard that was quite a rough storm Mr.

81
00:05:17.773 --> 00:05:18.614
Fogg experienced!

82
00:05:19.975 --> 00:05:21.536
I'm glad I missed that leg of the journey.

83
00:05:22.797 --> 00:05:24.118
I would have gotten terribly seasick.

84
00:05:25.018 --> 00:05:27.920
Imagine going through all that and then missing the Yokohama steamer.

85
00:05:29.421 --> 00:05:33.624
Yokohama was a small fishing village from the eighth century until the nineteenth century.

86
00:05:34.665 --> 00:05:39.909
In 1854 the Americans arrived with warships and demanded that Japan allow international trade.

87
00:05:40.709 --> 00:05:44.412
In response, the Japanese made Yokohama a port where foreign ships could dock.

88
00:05:45.852 --> 00:05:51.221
Raw silk, tea, and many other products were traded through Yokohama, and this little town was transformed into

89
00:05:51.241 --> 00:05:51.582
a city.

90
00:05:53.765 --> 00:06:00.296
Many Western influences first reached Japan through Yokohama, including the country's first daily newspaper and first railway.
